<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The iOS bundle's Info.plist, as a SOURCE file.

  It replaces the old ios/Info.plist, which was never actually used:
  scripts/build-ios.sh passed it as -DMACOSX_BUNDLE_INFO_PLIST=..., a
  cache variable, while CMakeLists.txt set the same name as a TARGET
  PROPERTY inside a bare `if(APPLE)` — and a target property beats a
  cache variable. Every iOS build therefore shipped macos/Info.plist.in:
  NSPrincipalClass=NSApplication, NSScreenCaptureUsageDescription, an
  .icns reference, and none of LSRequiresIPhoneOS /
  UILaunchStoryboardName / UISupportedInterfaceOrientations. That plist
  cannot be uploaded to App Store Connect.

  The same V-H4 rule as macOS applies here and is the reason this is a
  file and not a POST_BUILD edit: Qt decides at CONFIGURE time whether
  to link the static QDarwinMicrophonePermission / QDarwinCameraPermission
  backends, by running PlistBuddy over the file named by
  MACOSX_BUNDLE_INFO_PLIST. No usage-description key in this file at
  configure time means no plugin, which means every permission query
  answers Denied with no iOS prompt — and on iOS, unlike macOS, there is
  not even a Settings pane entry for the user to flip, because the app
  never asked. Nothing may go back to injecting these afterwards.

  @VAR@ placeholders are substituted by CMakeLists.txt's own
  configure_file(... @ONLY), from same-named variables.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>en</string>
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
	<string>BSFChat</string>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>@MACOSX_BUNDLE_EXECUTABLE_NAME@</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>@MACOSX_BUNDLE_GUI_IDENTIFIER@</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>BSFChat</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>

	<!-- Marketing version: what testers and the store listing show.
	     Must be M.N.P with no -rc suffix; Apple rejects anything else. -->
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>@MACOSX_BUNDLE_SHORT_VERSION_STRING@</string>
	<!-- Build number. Must strictly increase for every upload against a
	     given CFBundleShortVersionString, and a number is burned even by
	     an upload that is later rejected — so CI overrides this with the
	     workflow run number (-DBSFCHAT_BUILD_NUMBER=). Locally it falls
	     back to the version, which is fine because local builds never
	     reach App Store Connect. -->
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>@BSFCHAT_IOS_BUNDLE_VERSION@</string>

	<key>LSRequiresIPhoneOS</key>
	<true/>
	<key>UILaunchStoryboardName</key>
	<string>LaunchScreen</string>
	<!-- PORTRAIT ONLY, deliberately, for the first release.
	     ==================================================
	     This used to list LandscapeLeft and LandscapeRight as well, which
	     meant the app rotated — into a layout nobody had ever drawn.

	     There is no orientation-aware code anywhere in qml/: not one
	     binding reads Screen.orientation, primaryOrientation or compares
	     width against height. Every phone surface is a single vertical
	     stack sized for a portrait viewport, and the arithmetic in
	     landscape does not work out:

	       iPhone 15 landscape viewport        393 pt tall
	       − header                             48
	       − composer (56 min + its margins)    ~76
	       − software keyboard                 ~200
	       = message list                       ~69 pt — one row, or none.

	     Even with the keyboard down it is ~270 pt, and the drawers,
	     every Popup that clamps to `parent.height * 0.85`, and the
	     voice room's participant grid are all sized against a viewport
	     more than twice that tall. A reviewer who rotates the device is
	     doing something entirely ordinary, and what they would see is
	     a chat app with no chat in it.

	     Portrait-only is a normal shipping answer for a phone chat app,
	     it is honest about what has actually been laid out, and it is
	     reversible in this one array the day a landscape layout exists.
	     Android's half of this decision is android/AndroidManifest.xml's
	     screenOrientation.

	     Upside-down is still not listed: iPhone never auto-rotates to it,
	     and it would only matter on iPad, which we do not target
	     (TARGETED_DEVICE_FAMILY=1 in CMakeLists.txt). -->
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
	</array>

	<!-- ===== TCC usage descriptions =====
	     Apple rejects boilerplate here ("needs microphone access"): the
	     string has to name the feature and why it needs the hardware.
	     Each one is also the switch that makes Qt link the matching
	     permission plugin; see the note at the top of this file. -->
	<!-- The background clause is not padding. With UIBackgroundModes =
	     audio the microphone genuinely keeps running after the user
	     leaves the app, and a usage string that implies otherwise is
	     inaccurate in exactly the way App Review looks for. It also
	     answers the obvious reviewer question about why the background
	     mode is there at all. -->
	<key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
	<string>BSFChat uses your microphone so other people in a voice channel can hear you speak. Your microphone is only active while you are connected to a voice channel — including while BSFChat is in the background, so the call continues when you switch apps — and muting stops it.</string>
	<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
	<string>BSFChat uses your camera so you can turn your video on in a voice channel. The camera is only active while you choose to share video.</string>

	<!-- Local network. BSFChat is self-hosted: people routinely point it
	     at a server on their own LAN ("192.168.1.10:8448", a hostname
	     ending in .local, or a box running on the same machine). Since
	     iOS 14 any direct connection to a local-network address raises
	     the local-network prompt, and without this key iOS shows a
	     generic one and the connection is refused outright on the
	     stricter releases.

	     This becomes MANDATORY rather than merely correct as soon as
	     BSFCHAT_ENABLE_VOICE is turned on for iOS: voice is a
	     peer-to-peer mesh (src/voice/IVoiceTransport.h) whose ICE agent
	     gathers host candidates — the device's own LAN address — and
	     connects to peers directly unless the relay-only policy is in
	     force (src/voice/VoiceEngine.cpp buildRtcConfig).

	     Deliberately NOT declared: NSBonjourServices. There is no mDNS,
	     Bonjour, multicast or broadcast discovery anywhere in the client
	     — no QUdpSocket at all — so listing a service type would be a
	     declaration we do not use. -->
	<key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
	<string>BSFChat connects to the chat server you choose. Allow local network access if your server runs on this network — for example a server on your home or office LAN — so BSFChat can reach it.</string>

	<!-- App Transport Security.
	     NSAllowsLocalNetworking permits plain http:// ONLY to
	     local-network destinations (private IPv4 ranges, link-local,
	     .local names and unqualified hostnames). Public hosts still
	     require HTTPS. This is the key Apple explicitly does not ask you
	     to justify, unlike NSAllowsArbitraryLoads — which must never be
	     added here.

	     It is needed because a self-hosted server on a LAN commonly has
	     no publicly-trusted certificate, and the login field accepts
	     http:// exactly and only for that case (see normaliseServerUrl
	     in src/net/ServerDiscovery.cpp, and the "http://localhost:8448"
	     placeholder in qml/components/LoginDialog.qml). Without this,
	     every LAN server is unreachable from the iOS build with an
	     error that looks like the server being down. -->
	<key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
	<dict>
		<key>NSAllowsLocalNetworking</key>
		<true/>
	</dict>

	<!-- ===== Export compliance =====
	     Set explicitly so App Store Connect stops asking the encryption
	     questionnaire on EVERY upload, which is what turns a five-minute
	     TestFlight push into a manual gate.

	     YES, not NO, and the reasoning matters because a wrong answer
	     here is a declaration to the US government, not just to Apple:

	     Apple's exemption is for apps whose encryption is the operating
	     system's own (URLSession/Network.framework doing HTTPS). BSFChat
	     does not stay inside that line. The voice/video path is DTLS-SRTP
	     from libdatachannel with its vendored libsrtp, built against the
	     OpenSSL we cross-compile ourselves into deps/openssl-ios-* (see
	     cmake/Dependencies.cmake and scripts/build-openssl-ios.sh). That
	     is "industry-standard algorithms, not provided by Apple's OS",
	     which is the non-exempt column in Apple's own table — not the
	     proprietary column, so no CCATS is required, only the French
	     encryption declaration and only for distribution in France.

	     (Historic note: this key was set to YES while
	     BSFCHAT_ENABLE_VOICE was still OFF for iOS, on the reasoning
	     that an answer which silently goes stale between releases is
	     the failure mode the key exists to prevent. Voice turned on for
	     iOS on 2026-09-23, so the reasoning above is no longer
	     hypothetical — the shipped binary really does link DTLS-SRTP
	     against our own OpenSSL.)

	     CORRECTION to what an earlier version of this comment claimed:
	     setting this key YES does NOT stop App Store Connect asking.
	     It is NO that skips the questionnaire. YES means App Store
	     Connect will ask for export-compliance information — once per
	     version, not per build — until an ITSEncryptionExportComplianceCode
	     is pasted in here. Expect the questions; the honest answer is
	     still YES. docs/ios-release.md §3 has the walkthrough. -->
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<true/>

	<!-- Invite links. bsfchat://invite/... is delivered to the same
	     QFileOpenEvent filter as on macOS (src/core/UrlHandler.cpp);
	     without this the OS has nothing to route the scheme to and every
	     invite link a tester taps does nothing. Mirrors the
	     CFBundleURLTypes block in macos/Info.plist.in. -->
	<key>CFBundleURLTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleURLName</key>
			<string>com.bsfchat.url</string>
			<key>CFBundleURLSchemes</key>
			<array>
				<string>bsfchat</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>

	<!-- ===== Background modes =====
	     Substituted by CMakeLists.txt, and EMPTY unless
	     BSFCHAT_ENABLE_VOICE is on — because the honesty of this key is
	     what App Review checks. "audio" is legitimate only for an app
	     that really does keep capturing and playing while backgrounded;
	     declaring it in a build with voice compiled out is an unused
	     background mode, which is itself a rejection reason. Conversely,
	     with voice compiled in the key is MANDATORY: without it iOS
	     silences the session the moment the app leaves the foreground,
	     so every call would die when the user switched apps or locked
	     the screen.

	     What makes the declaration true rather than merely present is
	     src/voice/IosAudioSession.mm, which sets the AVAudioSession
	     category to playAndRecord with mode voiceChat. Those two things
	     ship together or not at all — a background mode with no
	     configured session is a rejection, and a configured session with
	     no background mode is a call that dies on backgrounding.

	     "voip" is never emitted. It requires a real PKPushRegistry +
	     CallKit integration and is rejected on sight without one; there
	     is no PushKit, no CallKit and no APNs anywhere in this client,
	     and notifications are local-only
	     (src/core/NotificationManager.h). -->
@BSFCHAT_IOS_BACKGROUND_MODES@
	<!-- ===== Deliberately absent, with reasons =====

	     NSPhotoLibraryUsageDescription / NSPhotoLibraryAddUsageDescription:
	     not declared because nothing touches the photo library. Both
	     attachment and avatar pickers are FileDialog
	     (qml/components/MessageInput.qml, UserSettings.qml), which maps
	     to UIDocumentPickerViewController — the Files app, no usage
	     string needed — and downloaded media is only ever written to the
	     app's own cache directory (src/core/MediaDownloader.cpp), never
	     to the camera roll. Adding a photo-library picker later means
	     adding the key AND the code path; iOS users will expect it.

	     NSScreenCaptureUsageDescription: macOS-only key. Screen sharing
	     on iOS needs ReplayKit and a Broadcast Upload Extension, which
	     is a separate target we have not built; the UI hides the control
	     rather than offering it (see qml/components/VoiceDock.qml).

	     NSFaceIDUsageDescription, NSContactsUsageDescription,
	     NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription: no such features exist.
	-->
</dict>
</plist>
